+### 3. Session Cookies, Cache, & Mail Links Across Forward Upgrades and Rollbacks
+
+- **Rails 6.1 <-> Rails 7.0 (`7.0` defaults)**:
+  - Kept `config.action_dispatch.cookies_serializer = :hybrid` temporarily during rollout so any unrefreshed Marshal session cookies from before the Rails 6.1 `:hybrid` deploy are read and migrated to JSON (readable by Rails 6.1 `:hybrid`, Rails 7.0, and Rails 7.2).
+  - Kept `config.active_support.cache_format_version = 6.1` when `Rails.gem_version < Gem::Version.new('7.1')` (Rails 7.2 removed support for `6.1`), so Rails 7.0 does not write `7.0` cache entries that Rails 6.1 cannot deserialize on rollback.
+  - Kept `config/initializers/upgrade_compatibility.rb` rotating `SHA1` KeyGenerator keys on Rails 7.0/7.2 (`SHA256` active) for `cookies_rotations.encrypted` (`aes-256-gcm` and `aes-256-cbc`), `cookies_rotations.signed`, and `:mail_links`, ensuring bidirectional compatibility with the deployed Rails 6.1 release.
+  - CSRF tokens: `load_defaults "7.0"` enables `urlsafe_csrf_tokens = true`. Ruby 3.2's `Base64.urlsafe_decode64` accepts both URL-safe and strict Base64 (`+`/`/`) CSRF tokens from Rails 6.1 sessions, and Rails 6.1 (`urlsafe_csrf_tokens = false`) accepts URL-safe CSRF tokens on rollback.
+
+- **Rails 7.0 (`7.0` defaults) <-> Rails 7.2 (`7.2` defaults) — Safe Rollback Target**:
+  - **Session cookies (`_redmine_session`)**:
+    - Both Rails 7.0 defaults and Rails 7.2 defaults use `OpenSSL::Digest::SHA256` for `active_support.key_generator_hash_digest_class`, `aes-256-gcm` (`use_authenticated_cookie_encryption = true`), `use_cookies_with_metadata = true` (`purpose: 'cookie._redmine_session'`), and JSON cookie serialization (`:json` or `:hybrid`).
+    - `ActionDispatch::Cookies` passes `NullSerializer` to `MessageEncryptor` / `MessageVerifier`, so `active_support.use_message_serializer_for_metadata = true` in Rails 7.2 defaults does **not** alter the encrypted/signed cookie metadata envelope (`{"_rails":{"message":...}}`). Session cookies written under Rails 7.2 defaults are directly readable on rollback to this Rails 7.0 release, and vice versa.
+  - **Mail links (`Rails.application.message_verifier(:mail_links)`)**:
+    - Rails 7.1/7.2 defaults set `active_support.message_serializer = :json_allow_marshal` and `active_support.use_message_serializer_for_metadata = true`.
+    - Under Rails 7.2 defaults, `Rails.application.message_verifier(:mail_links)` generates tokens using the Rails 7.1+ JSON metadata envelope `{"_rails":{"data":...,"exp":...,"pur":...}}` instead of Rails 7.0's legacy envelope `{"_rails":{"message":<base64 marshal>,"exp":...,"pur":...}}`. Vanilla Rails 7.0 `Messages::Metadata.verify` fails with `NoMethodError` (`Base64.strict_decode64(nil)`) on that format.
+    - Extended `config/initializers/upgrade_compatibility.rb` (for `Rails.gem_version < Gem::Version.new('7.1')`) so `Rails.application.message_verifier(:mail_links)` also verifies Rails 7.1/7.2 `{"_rails":{"data":...}}` envelopes (JSON or Marshal, strict or URL-safe Base64, primary or rotated keys, enforcing `exp` and `pur`), while continuing to generate Rails 7.0's legacy format (which Rails 7.2 `:json_allow_marshal` reads natively).
 
